# The default is "this is text, store it with LF". Everything below is either
# an exception to that or a merge rule.
#
# WHY A CATCH-ALL RATHER THAN A LIST OF TEXT TYPES
#
# Until 2026-09-05 this file listed the types that are text (`*.sh`, `*.py`,
# `*.md`, ...). Three things killed that shape, in increasing order of how hard
# they are to argue with:
#
#  1. It went stale exactly as predicted. `*.rs` and `*.toml` are 9 449 of the
#     13 907 tracked files and were declared by nothing, so 27 of them sat CRLF
#     in a worktree while `git status` called it clean. See
#     `known-issues.md` -> `A-27-KERNEL-SOURCES-ARE-CRLF-...` and
#     `design-decisions.md` 769.
#  2. **A list keyed on extensions cannot cover a file that has none, and the
#     most important script in this tree has none: `scripts/hooks/pre-push`.**
#     It is bash, it runs on every push, and `*.sh` does not match it. A CR in
#     it turns `set -u` into `set -u$'\r'` and the push gate dies in a way that
#     looks like the script being wrong -- the exact failure described further
#     down for the differential harnesses. No amount of maintaining the list
#     fixes this; the shape is wrong.
#  3. The repository's LF guarantee was resting on a file outside the
#     repository. `core.autocrlf=input` is set **only** at system scope, in
#     `C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig` -- installer-owned, untracked, and
#     not present on a fresh clone anywhere else. Every "the blobs are always
#     LF" claim in these documents was true by accident of one machine's git
#     installation. `text=auto` moves that guarantee into the repository, where
#     it is versioned and reviewed like everything else.
#
# `text=auto` asks git to decide text-vs-binary with its own NUL-byte scan.
# That heuristic is not trusted here -- `kernel/ada/.gitattributes` says so at
# length, and `known-issues.md` records it misfiring on a 4.5 MB document -- so
# it is not *relied* on: every binary in the tree is declared below, and every
# document that the heuristic is known to misjudge is declared further down.
# The heuristic only decides files in neither list, and the inversion is the
# point: the set of binary types here is small and nearly closed (22 files),
# while the set of text types grows with every commit.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The binaries, named rather than inferred. `kernel/ada/.gitattributes` makes
# the argument for this in full: git would almost certainly infer "binary" from
# the NUL bytes in these, "but that is an inference, and it is the sort that
# holds until the day it doesn't." A single translated byte in a disk image or
# a signed EFI binary is not a formatting problem, it is a boot failure.
# Spelled with the built-in `binary` macro, which expands to `-diff -merge
# -text`. All three matter: `-text` stops the conversion, `-diff` stops a
# textual diff of a PNG being printed as thousands of junk lines, and `-merge`
# stops git from ever attempting a three-way merge of two disk images, which
# cannot produce a valid one.
#
# These lines still match the `eol=lf` above, and `git check-attr` will show
# `eol: lf` on them. That is inert: once `text` is explicitly unset git takes
# the binary path and does no conversion at all, whatever `eol` says. Verified
# rather than assumed -- `git add --renormalize .` across all 13 907 tracked
# files stages exactly zero of them, these 22 included.
*.png  binary
*.deb  binary
*.fd   binary
*.efi  binary
*.EFI  binary
# Also declared in `kernel/ada/.gitattributes`, which wins for that directory.
# Repeated here so a `.o` committed anywhere else is covered too.
*.o    binary

# A shell script must arrive in the working tree with LF endings, on every
# platform and in every worktree.
#
# This is not a style preference. `bash` treats a CR as part of the token it
# ends, so a CRLF script fails in ways that look like the script being wrong:
# `set -u` becomes `set -u$'\r'` ("invalid option"), and `. "$(dirname
# "$0")/diff-wsl.sh"` looks for a file whose name ends in a carriage
# return. Twenty-three of this tree's differential harnesses were unrunnable
# from a Windows worktree for exactly that reason, and silently so — with
# `core.autocrlf=input` the CRLF is normalised away on commit, so `git status`
# stays clean and nothing ever points at the cause.
#
# `text eol=lf` says: normalise to LF in the repository (as `autocrlf=input`
# already did) *and* check out LF regardless of the platform or of any local
# `core.autocrlf`. The second half is the one that was missing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same reasoning for the other files that are read by a POSIX tool rather
# than by an editor: a CR inside them is data, not formatting.
*.py text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# The shared documents, for a different reason: to override git's guess.
#
# `core.autocrlf=input` normalises line endings only in files git believes are
# text, and its test is a scan of the whole blob for a NUL byte. `known-issues.md`
# had two, ~4.5 MB in, inside backtick spans quoting what `grep -Z` puts after a
# filename. So git called the file binary and stopped normalising it — and on
# 2026-08-25 an edit script that rewrote it through Python's default text mode
# (which on Windows turns `\n` into `\r\n`) committed 4.5 MB of CRLF with no
# warning and no visible diff hunk. The next merge saw every line as changed,
# conflicted on the entire file, and had no three-way to offer.
#
# `text eol=lf` is not a guess: it *asserts* these are text, so the NUL heuristic
# is not consulted. The NULs themselves are gone too — a document that quotes a
# control byte should spell it — but the attribute is the part that holds if one
# comes back. These files are the ones three lanes append to concurrently, which
# is exactly where an unmergeable 4.5 MB conflict is most expensive.
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf

# The two append-only JSONL logs, for the third variety of the same problem.
#
# Every lane's boot test appends one record to `boot-history.jsonl`, and every
# benchmark run one to `history.jsonl` -- always at end of file, never touching
# an existing line. Two lanes running in the same window therefore append at the
# same offset and git conflicts, on a machine-generated file that nobody should
# ever be hand-editing. Lane B hit it three times in one session
# (requests/b-a-boot-history-jsonl-conflicts-*).
#
# `merge=union` keeps both sides' lines instead of conflicting, which is always
# the right resolution here: each line is an independent record and neither side
# has modified the other's.
#
# It is only safe because the loaders sort. Union merge *concatenates* -- for a
# conflicting hunk it emits ours then theirs -- so the merged file's last line
# need not be the latest record, and both scripts read "most recent" out of file
# position (`tail_clean_streak` walks backwards from the end;
# `previous_for_host` returns `window[-1]`). `load_history` in each script now
# sorts by timestamp, which makes file order carry no meaning at all; that sort
# is load-bearing for these two lines and must not be "optimised away".
# `scripts/test-boot-history.py` asserts the property directly: a shuffled file
# must yield the same answers as a sorted one.
bench/boot-history.jsonl merge=union
bench/history.jsonl merge=union
